	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105011817916?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {In recent years, digital twin systems have created significant advancements in the field of healthcare, which provides learning and reasoning for improved decision-making using real-time data. However, existing chatbot framework for personalized feedback on depression symptoms have addressed challenges such as context-awareness, ambiguity and uncertainty. To overcome these challenges, this research improved a Chatbot framework by integrating Self-Attention Mechanism (SAM), which captures contextual relationships and enhances overall conversational experience. Initially, Extended Distress Analysis Interview Corpus (E-DAIC) served as foundational dataset, which includes semi clinical interviews. The Chatbot framework is designed on Rasa open-source architecture which utilizes pre-trained Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers (BERT) models. This BERT includes Natural Language Understanding (NLU) and core model in which, NLU model is responsible for capturing features including entities, intent, and contextual relationships. By integrating SAM into NLU model enhances representation learning through weighted feature importance, thereby enabling contextualized response generation by core model. From the results, the proposed Chatbot-SAM framework as dialogue system for detection of mental health problems achieved better results than existing Chatbot in terms of accuracy (0.74), precision (0.68), and recall (0.72).  © 2025 IEEE.},
