	abstract = {In today's technology-driven world, artificial intelligence (AI) has transformed human-computer interactions, with chatbots playing an essential role in automating customer support across various industries. The system conducts intent recognition and classification, which is the process of identifying a user's purpose based on natural language input. Accurate intent recognition requires context understanding, particularly in complex, ambiguous, or domain-specific conversations. We compared machine learning (ML) and deep learning (DL) models for intent classification, with an emphasis on context understanding of DL models. The ML models evaluated include Naive Bayes (NB), support vector machine (SVM), random forest classifier (RFC), gradient boosting machine (GBM), and extreme gradient boosting (XGBoost). The DL models included long short-term memory (LSTM) networks and the transformer-based bidirectional encoder representations from the transformers (BERT) model. The experiments were conducted on three datasets: SNIPS, CLINC150, and a medical intent dataset. The model performance was evaluated using traditional classification metrics such as accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score. Additionally, BERTScore was employed to assess semantic relevance and contextual understanding. The results indicated that DL models, particularly BERT, significantly outperformed traditional ML models in terms of classification. Furthermore, BERT demonstrated superior ability in capturing contextual semantics with enhanced generalization across different domains. The results highlight the effectiveness of advanced DL models in developing intelligent and context-aware dialogue systems, improving user experience in real-world applications. © 2025 IEEE.},
